{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 6156 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "377790b43fcd81b29839a545619750313b8a5be6a81267fd72f00169b83f2609" :edb/db-before-t 6155 :edb/db-after-t 6156 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539468 50 #inst "2026-10-24T02:21:05.788Z" 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186487323 1071 "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ buys $33%$ of the copies, and $90%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $2$ buys $25%$ of the copies, and $87%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ buys $42%$ of the copies, and $82%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $5%$ of all copies have some printing defect, and $70%$ of defective copies are sold, what is the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186487323 1071 "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ buys $33\\%$ of the copies, and $90\\%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $2$ buys $25\\%$ of the copies, and $87\\%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ buys $42\\%$ of the copies, and $82\\%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $5\\%$ of all copies have some printing defect, and $70\\%$ of defective copies are sold, what is the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186487323 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is bought by distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.33 &  & P(S|{D}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.87 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.42 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.33+0.25+0.42=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.05.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect, which is represented as $P(R|S).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.05)}{(0.9)(0.33)+(0.87)(0.25)+(0.82)(0.42)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.035}{0.8589} \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186487323 1074 "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is bought by distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.33 &  & P(S|{D}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.87 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.42 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.33+0.25+0.42=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $5\\%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.05.\n$$\n\n- Since $70\\%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen sold copy has a printing defect, which is represented as $P(R|S).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.05)}{(0.9)(0.33)+(0.87)(0.25)+(0.82)(0.42)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.035}{0.8589} \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186487326 1071 "Some researchers want to test the belief that teenagers who play an instrument are more likely to be good at math. They collect data on a large sample of teenagers that play an instrument.\n\n- $30%$ of teenagers play piano, and $50%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $20%$ of teenagers play violin, and $60%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $50%$ of teenagers play other instruments, and $47%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\nIf $10%$ of teenagers started to play music before $6$ years old, and $58%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, what is the probability that a randomly chosen teenager that is good at math started to play music before $6$ years old? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186487326 1071 "Some researchers want to test the belief that teenagers who play an instrument are more likely to be good at math. They collect data on a large sample of teenagers that play an instrument.\n\n- $30\\%$ of teenagers play piano, and $50\\%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $20\\%$ of teenagers play violin, and $60\\%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\n- $50\\%$ of teenagers play other instruments, and $47\\%$ of those teenagers are good at mathematics.\n\nIf $10\\%$ of teenagers started to play music before $6$ years old, and $58\\%$ of teenagers who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, what is the probability that a randomly chosen teenager that is good at math started to play music before $6$ years old? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186487326 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.5 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{2})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $58%$ of students who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.58.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who is good at math started to play before $6$ years old, which is represented as $P(B|M).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.58)(0.1)}{(0.5)(0.3)+(0.6)(0.2)+(0.47)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.058}{0.505} \\\\  & ≈0.115,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186487326 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that a teenager started to play music before $6$ years old, $M$ be the event that a teenager is good at math, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a teenager plays piano, violin, or another instrument, respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(M|{T}_{1})=0.5 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.2 &  & P(M|{T}_{2})=0.6 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.5 &  & P(M|{T}_{3})=0.47\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.2+0.5=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10\\%$ of teenagers started to play before $6$ years old, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $58\\%$ of students who started to play before $6$ years old are good at math, we have\n\n$$\nP(M|B)=0.58.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen teenager who is good at math started to play before $6$ years old, which is represented as $P(B|M).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|M) & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(M|B)P(B)}{P(M|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(M|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(M|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.58)(0.1)}{(0.5)(0.3)+(0.6)(0.2)+(0.47)(0.5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.058}{0.505} \\\\  & ≈0.115,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186487334 1071 "Paul purchased his land for $400$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10%$ each year. How much will Paul's plot be worth after $5$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186487334 1071 "Paul purchased his land for $400$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10\\%$ each year. How much will Paul's plot be worth after $5$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186487334 1074 "The land initially costs $400$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10%$ per year.\n\n$10%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $5$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{5}$ thousand dollars \n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $5$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n400\\cdot (1.1)^{5}=644.204≈644,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately $644$ thousand dollars." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186487334 1074 "The land initially costs $400$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10\\%$ per year.\n\n$10\\%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars\n\n* After $5$ years, the value will be $400\\cdot (1.1)^{5}$ thousand dollars \n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $5$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n400\\cdot (1.1)^{5}=644.204≈644,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $5$ years, the land will be worth approximately $644$ thousand dollars." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186487354 1071 "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $44%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $70%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $25%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $85%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $31%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3.$\n\nIf $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, and $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186487354 1071 "A company is selling a new product on three e-commerce platforms, platform $1,$ platform $2,$ and platform $3.$\n\n- $44\\%$ of customers bought the product on platform $1,$ and $70\\%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $25\\%$ of customers bought the product on platform $2,$ and $85\\%$ of those customers were satisfied with the product.\n\n- $31\\%$ of customers bought the product on platform $3.$\n\nIf $25\\%$ of all customers are teenagers, $60\\%$ of teenage customers are satisfied with the product, and $20\\%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186487354 1074 "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.7 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.44+0.25+0.31=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $60%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.6.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of satisfied customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|S)=0.2.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen customer from platform $3$ is satisfied with the product, which is represented as $P(S|{P}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S|{P}_{1})P({P}_{1})+P(S|{P}_{2})P({P}_{2})+P(S|{P}_{3})P({P}_{3})} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{(0.60)(0.25)}{(0.7)(0.44)+(0.85)(0.25)+P(S|{P}_{3})(0.31)} \\\\ 0.2 & =\\frac{0.15}{0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{P}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.2(0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3})) & =0.15 \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.15}{0.2} \\\\ 0.5205+0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 0.31P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205 \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.31}(\\frac{3}{4}-0.5205) \\\\ P(S|{P}_{3}) & ≈0.740\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186487354 1074 "Let $T$ be the event that a customer is a teenager, $S$ be the event that a customer is satisfied, and ${P}_{1},{P}_{2},{P}_{3}$ be the event that a customer bought the product on platform $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({P}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(S|{P}_{1})=0.7 \\\\  & P({P}_{2})=0.25 &  & P(S|{P}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({P}_{3})=0.31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${P}_{1}⊔{P}_{2}⊔{P}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({P}_{1})+P({P}_{2})+P({P}_{3})=0.44+0.25+0.31=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $25\\%$ of all customers are teenagers, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $60\\%$ of teenage customers are satisfied, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|T)=0.6.\n$$\n\n- Since $20\\