y bought $8$ small cakes for $\\$12.$ At what unit price did Lily pay?" 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186490293 1071 "John bought a bouquet of $4$ roses for $$5.$ What is the price per rose?" 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186490293 1071 "John bought a bouquet of $4$ roses for $\\$5.$ What is the price per rose?" 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186490293 1074 "The ratio of dollars to roses is $5:4.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $25:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{125}{100}=1.25\n$$\n\nConsequently, the cost is $$1.25$ per rose." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186490293 1074 "The ratio of dollars to roses is $5:4.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $25:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{125}{100}=1.25\n$$\n\nConsequently, the cost is $\\$1.25$ per rose." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186490297 1071 "John bought $25$ candies for $$3.$ What is the price per candy?" 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186490297 1071 "John bought $25$ candies for $\\$3.$ What is the price per candy?" 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186490297 1074 "The ratio of dollars to candies is $3:25.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{12}{100}=0.12\n$$\n\nConsequently, the cost is $$0.12$ per candy." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186490297 1074 "The ratio of dollars to candies is $3:25.$ Expressing this as a fraction, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{25}\n$$\n\nWe can convert this fraction to a decimal by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{12}{100}=0.12\n$$\n\nConsequently, the cost is $\\$0.12$ per candy." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186491116 1071 "The price of a gallon of gasoline is $$2.$ Some economists estimate that its price will rise by $2%$ weekly. What will be the price of a gallon of gasoline after $4$ weeks?" 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186491116 1071 "The price of a gallon of gasoline is $\\$2.$ Some economists estimate that its price will rise by $2\\%$ weekly. What will be the price of a gallon of gasoline after $4$ weeks?" 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186491116 1074 "The price of a gallon of gasoline is $$2$, and this amount will increase by $2%$ each week.\n\n$2%$ as a decimal is $0.02.$ Therefore, an increase of $2%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.02=1.02.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ week, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{4}$ dollars." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186491116 1074 "The price of a gallon of gasoline is $\\$2$, and this amount will increase by $2\\%$ each week.\n\n$2\\%$ as a decimal is $0.02.$ Therefore, an increase of $2\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.02=1.02.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ week, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ weeks, the price will be $2\\cdot (1.02)^{4}$ dollars." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186491120 1071 "Max initially had $$2$ in his piggy bank. Then, thanks to a generous relative, the amount of money in Max's piggy bank doubled at the end of every day. How much money will Max have in the piggy bank after $6$ days?" 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186491120 1071 "Max initially had $\\$2$ in his piggy bank. Then, thanks to a generous relative, the amount of money in Max's piggy bank doubled at the end of every day. How much money will Max have in the piggy bank after $6$ days?" 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186491120 1074 "Initially, Max has $$2$ in the piggy bank, and this amount doubles every day.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ day, Max has $2\\cdot 2$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- $…$\n\n- After $6$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{6}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for its value after $6$ days, we get:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot (2)^{6}=2\\cdot 64=128\n$$\n\nThus, Max will have $$128$ after $6$ days." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186491120 1074 "Initially, Max has $\\$2$ in the piggy bank, and this amount doubles every day.\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- After $1$ day, Max has $2\\cdot 2$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- $…$\n\n- After $6$ days, Max will have $2\\cdot (2)^{6}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for its value after $6$ days, we get:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot (2)^{6}=2\\cdot 64=128\n$$\n\nThus, Max will have $\\$128$ after $6$ days." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186491124 1071 "Mary is randomly picking a candy from a bag and putting it back in until she gets a lemon-flavored candy. Approximately, $6%$ of the candies in the bag are lemon-flavored. What is the probability that the $9$th candy is the first lemon-flavored candy picked? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186491124 1071 "Mary is randomly picking a candy from a bag and putting it back in until she gets a lemon-flavored candy. Approximately, $6\\%$ of the candies in the bag are lemon-flavored. What is the probability that the $9$th candy is the first lemon-flavored candy picked? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186491124 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained. Since the chance to get a lemon-flavored candy is $6%,$ the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=6%=\\frac{6}{100}=0.06.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.06).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.06)^{x-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{x-1}(0.06)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $9$th attempt is the first lemon-flavored candy is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=9) & =f(9) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{9-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{8}(0.06) \\\\  & ≈0.037\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186491124 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained. Since the chance to get a lemon-flavored candy is $6\\%,$ the number of picked candies until a lemon-flavored candy is obtained can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=6\\%=\\frac{6}{100}=0.06.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.06).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.06)^{x-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{x-1}(0.06)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $9$th attempt is the first lemon-flavored candy is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=9) & =f(9) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{9-1}(0.06) \\\\  & =(0.94)^{8}(0.06) \\\\