 function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.05$ | $0.10$ | $0.15$ | $0.20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ | $\\,0.0336\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ | $\\,0.0418\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n\nApprximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee." 13194139539468 17592186576411 true] [17592186493975 1074 "Let $X$ represent the amount of coffee in a randomly selected cup. Then we have $X∼N(10,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<8.5).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nWe know that $P(X<9.1)=0.15.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<9.1) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{9.1-10}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.15 \\\\ \\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-0.9}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{0.9}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.9}{1.0364} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈0.8684.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<8.5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<8.5) & =P(X\\le 8.5) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{8.5-10}{0.8684}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.73) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.73) \\\\  & =0.0418 \\\\  & ≈4.18%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $4.18%$ of coffee cups contain less than $8.5$ ounces of coffee." 13194139539468 17592186576411 false] [17592186493975 1074 "Let $X$ represent the amount of co